edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508006 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508006 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ce85fa0-5910-5394-8fe4-97d58b71482a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508012 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b4405dc2-f699-573c-8e67-1be9feb770b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508018 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508018 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508018 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508018 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508018 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c61573f5-b18b-5585-9962-5cb9203f87fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508038 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508038 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2},$ where $-2<x\\le 5.$ What is its range?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "047624c0-9a25-551c-ac1f-663285e0168d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508038 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/57/57f5c6e193c1db7dc05024088bdcae5cef890c252eb0c06621e36174e1353a0e.png)\n\nThen, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1.$ This occurs at the point $(-2,-1).$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $\\frac{5}{2}.$ This occurs at the point $(5,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\frac{5}{2},$ including $\\frac{5}{2}$ but not including $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1<y\\le \\frac{5}{2},$ which can also be written as  \n\n$$\n-1<f(x)\\le \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508049 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/3948e71958b683ca5840744785f5480ab7f2a15231c615c5d16f85a681cd7650.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14980038-fb7b-5e0f-91bc-a2f1f2823387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508049 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1.$ This occurs when $x\\to -\\infty .$ However, note  that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\infty ,$ but not including $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1<y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as  $-1<f(x)<\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-235981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}-1,$ where $x$ is any real number. What is its range?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}\n\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bd1c83e-29b0-58c6-bbc9-6066b2370a02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508051 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/de/de9f7bcc43c626c12da1dde90660c0a231bb6ad9320b8e2c08cfbd1241d034d0.png)\n\nThe graph will cover all $y$-values, so the range of the function is $-\\infty <y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as\n\n$$\n-\\infty <f(x)<\\infty .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129371" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508055 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c0/c0cb7662243945ddd6cc4bb8207a6678dd8b6dae04fb4081f90e9a3c47737073.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "341d3d66-0ff6-50ab-9a65-6acf05b4fdd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508055 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- There is no smallest $y$-value.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $2.$ This occurs when $x=1.$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-\\infty $ and $2,$ including $2.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-\\infty <y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as  $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508089 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b0b5c2160ca027b6acf752dfb4a31725d312b06e5c27127fcd66b7346eeee31.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1d00aa5-c66d-5b2f-86d7-c93af95cd3b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508089 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-3,1]$ is $[-2,1]$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (1,3)$ is $(-3,1)$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-2,1]\\cup (-3,1)=(-3,1].$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the value $y=-3$ is NOT a valid output of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use parentheses $(…)$ instead of brackets $[…]$ at this endpoint." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
